      "text": "Answer:\n$\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6] \\cup (-1, \\infty)}$\nSince the quantity in the numerator of the inequality is always positive, only the denominator matter when trying to solve the inequality. So we need to determine what values make that quantity partly or fully negative to make the inequality negative.\n\nTo determine that, we set the quantity equal to zero:\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 + 2x + 7 &= 0.\n\\end{align*}To solve this, we can either solve the quadratic equation or simplify the expression as a perfect square (attempting to \"introduce\" negative numbers). However, the latter would only make the values negative  and  even if multiplied to yield  . So let's continue with the former. We start by completing the square:\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 + 2x + 7 &= 0 \\\\\nx^2 + 2x &= - 7 \\\\\n(x+1)^2 - 1 & = - 7 \\\\\n(x+1)^2 &= - 6\n\\end{align*}Since the quantity is inside a square, and squares are always nonnegative, the quantity cannot be negative. Also, since we are not dealing with an imaginary number on the inside, it cannot have a negative value inside, since the powers of imaginary numbers is  . However, we can still solve this equation since the only values for it are either 0 or negative  . Therefore it always makes sense to graph the value  to see where it is satisfied.\n\nWe found that it's a solution, so that solves for one of the isolated variables. Then the inequality becomes:\n\\begin{align*}\n(x + 6) \\left( x^2 + 2x + 7 \\right) &\\ge 0 \\\\\nx^3 + 8x^2 + 25x + 42 &= x^3 + 8x^2 + 25x + 25 + 17 \\\\\n(x+6)^2 (x+7) &= (x+6)(x+7) + 17\n\\end{align*}Let's graph that. First, we graph the expression and then some other numbers. \n\nWe see that it graphed nicely with a hole in the middle at  . However, what's inside that hole is just some huge insulated material with couplings that rarely ever need one of that size. \n\nIn the end, we confirm that there is a solution between  and  . The answer is $\\boxed{[-6, -1]}$, or our original answer  scheme