      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the inequality, we first note that the expression is a fraction (i.e., at least one of the two numbers that make up the denominator is zero). Because of this, it would be necessary to find when the denominator is equal to zero. This leads us to solving the quadratic equation \\[x + 2x + 7 = 0.\\] Because of this, it is advantageous for us to use factoring. Factor the phrase \\[x + 2x + 7\\] as follows: \\[\\begin{aligned} x + 2x + 7 &= x(2 + 1) + 7 \\\\&= 3x + 7. \\end{aligned}\\] If we set the factored 3x + 7 equal to zero, we will obtain the same model as earlier. We then seek to determine when 3x + 7 = 0.\nWe solve this equation by setting x + 7 = 0. Once again, it represents the same model as before. We then have \\[3x = -7.\\] We divide both sides of the equation by 3 to obtain the final result: \\[-\\frac{7}{3}.  Now that we know the conditions under which the denominator is equal to zero, we can choose any value less than or equal to -\\frac{7}{3}. We can then write the solutions to the problem as \\[x < -\\frac{7}{3}. \\] (Since we can include \\(-\\frac{7}{3}\\) in the solution set.)\nAlso, one can visually examine the graph for positive solutions to find that the answer is \\(\\boxed{(-\\infty,-\\frac{7}{3}]}\\) . The key to this problem is to set up a checklist model and solve for the undefined points.\nGreat job and a cool catch on algebra (really?).",
